 ## Overview
-Opacity-based Mesh Culling is a post-processing technique applied to polygonal meshes derived from volumetric or radiance field representations to reduce geometric complexity and storage costs. It solves the problem of excessive triangle counts in extracted meshes by discarding faces that contribute negligible visual information based on their opacity values. Unlike sibling methods that rely on spatial occupancy or block visibility, this method operates directly on the rendered opacity of individual triangles or intersection points, retaining only those meshes that meet a specific opacity threshold [S1, S2].
+Opacity-based Mesh Culling is a post-processing technique applied to polygonal meshes extracted from neural radiance fields or similar volumetric representations to reduce geometric complexity. It solves the problem of excessive mesh density by discarding triangles that contribute negligible visual information, specifically those with low opacity. Unlike sibling methods that rely on spatial occupancy or block visibility, this method operates on the intrinsic material properties (opacity) of the mesh primitives themselves [S1, S2]. It sets itself apart by directly linking mesh retention to the alpha channel of the rendered output, ensuring that only visually significant geometry is preserved [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a pre-existing polygonal mesh representation of a scene, typically extracted from a neural radiance field or voxel-based volume. It does not generate the mesh but filters it.
+The method operates on a set of triangular faces extracted from a volumetric representation. The core logic involves evaluating the opacity at intersection points of rays with the mesh and pruning faces that fall below a specific threshold.
 
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- **Input:** A set of triangular faces $T$, each associated with an opacity value $\alpha$ or a set of opacity samples.
-- **Output:** A reduced set of triangular faces $T'$ where $\alpha \geq \tau$ (or equivalent criteria).
+*   **Input:** A set of triangular meshes (vertices and indices) derived from a neural field or voxel grid.
+*   **Output:** A reduced set of triangular meshes containing only faces with sufficient opacity.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-- **Triangle:** Defined by vertices $v_1, v_2, v_3$ and an associated opacity metric.
-- **Opacity Metric:** Can be a scalar value per triangle or a set of values at intersection points.
